---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RateLimitCalculator from '../../components/RateLimitCalculator.jsx';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="API Rate Limit Calculator"
  description="Calculate how much concurrency you need and how long a batch job will take under a given API rate limit."
>
  <RateLimitCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why rate limit math matters</h2>
  <p>
    Hitting a 429 (rate limited) error mid-batch-job is one of the most
    common surprises when working with AI APIs. Knowing your required
    concurrency and expected completion time up front lets you plan retries,
    backoff, and job scheduling correctly.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your API's rate limit in requests per minute (check your provider's dashboard).</li>
    <li>Enter your average response time in milliseconds.</li>
    <li>Enter how many total requests your batch job needs to send.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>API Rate Limit Calculator FAQ</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>What is Little's Law?</strong> A queuing theory formula stating
    that the number of concurrent requests in flight equals throughput
    multiplied by average latency — it's the standard way to estimate
    required concurrency for a target request rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Should I run at exactly the rate limit?</strong> No — leave 10-20%
    headroom. Running right at the limit risks intermittent 429 errors from
    timing jitter.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What happens if I exceed the calculated concurrency?</strong>
    You'll start hitting 429 rate-limit errors more frequently — the
    calculated concurrency is the safe ceiling for your target throughput,
    not a hard technical limit enforced by this tool.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Does this account for retries?</strong> No — it calculates
    baseline concurrency for first-attempt requests only; retries after a
    rate-limit error add additional load on top of this baseline.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
